        <article class="doc-card">
            <header class="doc-header">
                <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
                <p class="doc-meta">Effective 26 August 2026 &middot; SACC Software Limited</p>
            </header>

            <p class="doc-lede">
                This is the agreement between you (or the organization you're signing up on behalf of) and SACC
                Software Limited for the use of Q-Mgr. Registering an organization, or clicking "I agree" on the
                sign-up form, means you're accepting it — so it's worth actually reading, and we've tried to
                write it so that's not a chore.
            </p>

            <h2>1. Who can use Q-Mgr</h2>
            <p>
                You need to be at least 18 and have real authority to sign your organization up — a business
                owner, an IT admin, an operations manager, whoever's actually responsible for making this call.
                If you're setting this up on behalf of a company, "you" throughout this document means that
                company, and you're confirming you're allowed to bind it to these terms.
            </p>

            <h2>2. What Q-Mgr actually is</h2>
            <p>
                Q-Mgr is a queue and visitor management platform: ticket-based queuing across service counters,
                a Visitor Management module for front-desk check-in and badges, digital signage for waiting-area
                screens, a Campaign Marketing tool for SMS/email/WhatsApp/Telegram outreach to your own contacts,
                and the reporting that ties it together. Every organization gets its own isolated space — your
                data, your branches, your users — even though we all run on the same platform underneath.
            </p>

            <h2>3. Your account and your trial</h2>
            <p>
                New organizations start on a free trial with no card required up front &mdash; the current trial
                length is always shown on the registration page itself, since it's the kind of number that
                changes more often than this document should. During the trial
                you can use the plan's features without being charged; if the trial ends without an active paid
                subscription, the account moves to a restricted state rather than being deleted outright — your
                data stays put, you just can't keep operating day-to-day until you either subscribe or reach out
                to us. You're responsible for keeping your login credentials to yourself and for what happens
                under your account, so use a real password and don't share logins between staff who should each
                have their own.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Plans, billing, and cancelling</h2>
            <p>
                We offer a Free tier and paid Starter, Professional, and Enterprise plans, each with different
                limits on branches, staff seats, and monthly ticket volume — the current pricing and limits are
                shown on the billing page inside the app, since those numbers change more often than this
                document should. Paid subscriptions are billed monthly or annually through Stripe, in advance.
                If a payment fails and isn't resolved, we'll suspend account access rather than let usage run up
                against an unpaid bill — you'll see exactly why, with a way to fix it, not just a dead login
                page. You can cancel a paid subscription at any time from the billing page; you keep access
                through the end of the period you've already paid for, and we don't do partial refunds for
                unused time unless something on our end genuinely went wrong.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Using it responsibly</h2>
            <p>You agree not to:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Use Q-Mgr for anything illegal, or to harass, defraud, or discriminate against anyone.</li>
                <li>Send Campaign Marketing messages to people who haven't given you a real reason to expect them — a past customer, a patient, someone who opted in. The unsubscribe link on every message is there so recipients can opt out; removing it, ignoring opt-outs, or buying a contact list to blast is a violation of these terms and, in most places, the law. That's on you as the sender, not on us.</li>
                <li>Use the Visitor Management watchlist feature to flag people for discriminatory reasons. It exists for genuine security purposes — a documented past incident, a real safety concern — not as a way to informally blacklist someone you'd rather not deal with.</li>
                <li>Try to break tenant isolation, access another organization's data, or probe the platform for vulnerabilities without telling us first (we'd rather you email us about a security issue than exploit it — see Section 10).</li>
                <li>Resell or sublicense access to the platform itself without a separate written agreement with us.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                If we find an account doing any of the above, we can suspend or terminate it. We'd rather warn
                you and give you a chance to fix it — abuse serious enough for an instant termination is the
                exception, not our default move.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Whose data it is</h2>
            <p>
                Everything you or your staff put into Q-Mgr — your visitor logs, your queue history, your
                contact lists, your branding — is yours. We don't claim ownership of it, and we don't use it for
                anything beyond running the service you're paying us for and, in aggregate and de-identified
                form, understanding how the platform gets used so we can improve it. If your account is
                cancelled, your data is retained for a short recovery window and then deleted along with any
                associated visitor/customer records, following the same retention rules described in our
                Privacy Policy.
            </p>

            <h2>7. Uptime and things breaking</h2>
            <p>
                We run Q-Mgr to be available and reliable, and we take outages seriously when they happen. We're
                not going to promise a specific uptime percentage with financial penalties attached here — that
                kind of formal SLA is something Enterprise customers can discuss with us directly, not a blanket
                claim we'll make to everyone up front. What we will say plainly: if the service is down, we're
                already working on it, and we'll tell you what happened once we know.
            </p>

            <h2>8. Ending the relationship</h2>
            <p>
                You can close your account whenever you like from the billing page, or by emailing us. We can
                suspend or terminate an account for non-payment, a real breach of Section 5, or if we're required
                to by law — and where it's practical to do so without making the underlying problem worse, we'll
                give notice and a chance to fix things first. On termination, access stops, and your data follows
                the retention and deletion rules described in our Privacy Policy rather than disappearing
                instantly.
            </p>

            <h2>9. Liability</h2>
            <p>
                Q-Mgr is provided as-is. We work to keep it accurate and available, but we can't promise it will
                be error-free or uninterrupted, and to the extent the law allows, we're not liable for indirect
                or consequential losses — lost business, lost data from something outside our control, that sort
                of thing. Nothing here limits liability that can't legally be limited, like liability for our own
                gross negligence or willful misconduct.
            </p>

            <h2>10. Found a security issue?</h2>
            <p>
                Tell us before you tell anyone else: <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
                We won't pursue action against a good-faith report made responsibly, and we genuinely want to
                know before a real problem finds us the hard way.
            </p>

            <h2>11. Changes to these terms</h2>
            <p>
                If we change these terms in a way that meaningfully affects how you use Q-Mgr, we'll update the
                effective date above and email account admins directly. Continuing to use the service after that
                means you've accepted the update; if you don't agree with a change, you can close your account
                before it takes effect.
            </p>

            <h2>12. Governing law</h2>
            <p>
                These terms are governed by the laws of the Republic of Uganda. Any dispute that can't be worked
                out directly between us will be handled under that jurisdiction.
            </p>

            <h2>13. Contact</h2>
            <p>
                Questions about any of this: <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
            </p>

